# GX10 node stack. Identical on every node — nothing here needs to differ,
# because the agent derives its node id from the host's hostname.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# OPTIONAL. Left unset, the agent uses the host's hostname (read from the
# mounted procfs, so it's the host's — not the container's). That's what lets
# one stack repo deploy unchanged to all three GX10s.
#
# Set it only if a node's hostname is a poor label. It becomes the `node` label
# on every metric, so keep it stable — changing it orphans that node's history.
# NODE_ID=gx10-1

# Published to the Forgejo container registry by scripts/publish-images.sh,
# which pushes BOTH a sha tag and :latest.
#
# :latest is the intended steady state — Dockhand pulls new images once a day
# in off-hours, so the stack converges without a git change (roadmap.md, open
# decision 6). Until Dockhand is actually driving this stack, rollout is manual
# and pinning the sha is what makes a deploy happen.
#
# Pinning also stays useful afterwards, as the exception path: when a bad build
# lands overnight, pin the last-good sha and redeploy without rebuilding.
#
# Note :latest means configuration no longer records what is deployed — that
# job belongs to sparkdash_agent_build_info, which reports what actually ran.
AGENT_IMAGE=forgejo.indielab.tech/brian/spark-dash-agent:latest

# llama.cpp router base URLs, comma-separated — a node commonly runs several
# router containers. Leave blank on a node that runs only vLLM.
#
# Use the host's address, not a container name: this stack runs on its own
# bridge network and deliberately doesn't join the inference containers'
# network, so it has no external dependency that must exist first.
#   LLAMA_ROUTER_URLS=http://192.168.50.61:8001,http://192.168.50.61:8108
LLAMA_ROUTER_URLS=

# Routers where per-model metrics may be fetched. LEAVE EMPTY unless you're
# sure: a /metrics?model= request LOADS the model on an autoload router. Model
# names, states and capacity are reported for every router regardless — this
# only controls the extra per-model throughput/KV-cache detail.
# Opt in one router at a time, and never one hosting models large enough to
# exhaust the shared pool.
#   LLAMA_METRICS_ROUTERS=http://192.168.50.61:8001
LLAMA_METRICS_ROUTERS=

# Comma-separated vLLM /metrics endpoints on this node, e.g.
#   VLLM_URLS=http://vllm-70b:8000/metrics,http://vllm-8b:8001/metrics
VLLM_URLS=

# Temperature bands. LEAVE THESE UNSET — the agent reads the real limits from
# the hardware and derives its bands from them:
#
#   GPU  from NVML's slowdown threshold (86C on GB10; shutdown is 90C), so
#        critical lands where the GPU actually starts throttling itself.
#   CPU  from the thermal zone's `critical` trip (104C on the GX10), which is
#        where the kernel powers the machine off.
#
# `nvidia-smi` prints the GPU thresholds as N/A — they're only visible through
# NVML — so there is no easy way to check these by hand. The agent logs both at
# startup, and reports them as sparkdash_{gpu,cpu}_temp_{warning,critical}_celsius.
#
# Set these ONLY to override a node whose derived values are wrong. Hardcoded
# bands are how this went wrong before: an 80C critical fired during ordinary
# ComfyUI work, while a 94C alert sat above the 90C at which the GPU powers
# itself off and so could never fire at all.
#
# Both halves of a pair are required; setting one alone is ignored with a
# warning, since a hand-set warning paired with a derived critical produces two
# numbers that don't relate to each other.
#   TEMP_WARNING_C=82
#   TEMP_CRITICAL_C=86
#   CPU_TEMP_WARNING_C=92
#   CPU_TEMP_CRITICAL_C=98

LOG_LEVEL=INFO
